{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"e06fd69b56ff3dd2af73e985ba4b1e3b6b7d5424a443de545534a5f60a50aa47","created_at":"2026-08-22T22:20:29+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"90047852d9f077e2ac02d75595f29855d3ad6518d5458d27a8f8803b13cd3f96","input_tokens":5895,"latency_ms":24984,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"883634ef2a4ca2b24e0b058da88a5ff1ba823f8a83a23d95e57f7121443440e4","output_tokens":2388,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:3640b70becd75147dde99afe","meta_principle_id":"meta:foundations:robustness-sensitivity","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:scientific-discovery:01M0NRYH3RF96HQZZBJ6MPK5E5","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle holds that credible claims should survive plausible perturbations in measurement, preprocessing, or parameterization, and that sensitivity analysis maps which conclusions are stable. The principle is a direct instance of this: replacing truncation with an n-th percentile is a plausible change in error aggregation, and the finding that RMSE magnitude decreases while rankings and conclusions remain stable is exactly the kind of sensitivity result the meta-principle describes.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:06:05+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T22:20:29+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.58,"content_digest":"3a5f9f78ca8cbfa7142f302abb26d3d7a07dc1c9786c760233688a013ad25aba","created_at":"2026-08-22T16:30:05+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"f5d5d4ee3bd8059f02caa08cbf8d36686d429204ea0bea04aa68a825dd62577a","input_tokens":4074,"latency_ms":34877,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"254d4907753a859cb6e2244977d440dd22375426a0e8ac5255cbe7453e0cffaf","output_tokens":2369,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:57760858f0001e72e443f4c6","meta_principle_id":"meta:neuroscience-cognition:attractor-dynamics","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0N4WKWY46ZTT46N39JQF4D1","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"CPG-generated stereotypical locomotion is a recurrent-network activity pattern, and the meta-principle explicitly covers motor control via stable or metastable attractors; the principle's modulation by cognitive and sensory inputs is compatible with input-driven attractor dynamics.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T16:30:05+00:00","variable_compatibility":"partial"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.92,"content_digest":"139941479e47e66db526cac6c2f34a27326d2bff44d7cbaed442ac6d44556499","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:58:48+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"1b60335a2d504c9c5a7be6b4b41a96fe19209210fca9ee1a82651a0c86e6cd9b","input_tokens":5799,"latency_ms":27801,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"e01bb8fe75c3751230a5c00fe3d48aca39d958fba872d0712506d1cfc320e483","output_tokens":2423,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:b8a4fdf370ffb516fce02668","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:cook-levin-np-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NDD4NWHEB5BGPN57DJBJFA","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The NP-completeness meta-principle explicitly states that NP-hardness is a worst-case statement and does not preclude efficient algorithms on structured instances. The observed practical solvability of vision-derived graph matching instances is therefore conceptually supported by this boundary, not contradicted by it.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:06:05+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:58:48+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.9,"content_digest":"273ef77f3f27435b1feaa739564a2b533f2a2a1ca4657bac321fefa931d2bc9e","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:23+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:e6a121b5d373c64251f538b1","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:interdisciplinary-science:01M0HHZFCW9EWVR511F1QX5H7H","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim uses LSBS's EXP-completeness to infer that membership of LSBS in NP would imply EXP = NP. This is exactly the completeness-based class-collapse argument: an EXP-complete problem in NP places EXP inside NP, and since NP is contained in EXP, the classes coincide. The complexity-class scale and completeness conditions are compatible.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:23+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
